<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE suite SYSTEM "https://testng.org/testng-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    ============================================================================
    REVISION NOTES — MODULE 7: Tag-based suite strategy — SMOKE suite
    ============================================================================
    IMPORTANT NUANCE: this XML file does NOT filter by tag itself — TestNG
    has no native concept of Cucumber tags. The ACTUAL tag filtering
    happens via a system property Cucumber's own runtime reads
    automatically, with priority over anything hardcoded in @CucumberOptions:

        mvn test -DsuiteXmlFile=testng-smoke.xml -Dcucumber.filter.tags="@smoke"

    So this file's real job is just: "run the SAME TestRunner class, but
    intended to be paired with a @smoke tag filter, with a thread count
    tuned for a FAST pipeline stage." Smoke suites are typically small
    (a handful of critical-path scenarios) and run on EVERY commit for
    fast feedback — so we don't need many threads; low overhead matters
    more than raw parallelism here.

    ENTERPRISE PATTERN THIS SETS UP FOR MODULE 10:
      Jenkins pipeline stage "Smoke Test" (runs on every PR/commit) uses
      THIS file + @smoke tag filter. A separate nightly "Full Regression"
      Jenkins job uses testng-regression.xml + @regression tag filter with
      a much higher thread count, since it runs on a schedule where total
      wall-clock time matters more than resource frugality.
    ============================================================================
-->
<suite name="SmokeSuite" data-provider-thread-count="2" verbose="1">

    <listeners>
        <listener class-name="com.company.automation.listeners.AnnotationTransformer"/>
        <listener class-name="com.company.automation.listeners.ExtentReportListener"/>
    </listeners>

    <test name="SmokeTests">
        <classes>
            <class name="com.company.automation.runners.TestRunner"/>
        </classes>
    </test>

</suite>
